IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `clatas-dates`
IMPORT `clatas-common`

-- Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) -- whether the defendant can be held liable: the tests and defences a claimant meets.
-- Part 4 s 7 (apologies), Part 6 (breach of duty, causation, obvious risks, dangerous recreational activities,
-- professional negligence), Part 8 (mental harm), Part 8A (good samaritans), Part 8B (food donors), Part 9 (public and
-- other authorities), Part 9A (proportionate liability), Part 10 (volunteers), Part 10A (accommodation providers), Part
-- 10B (employer and employee). Asked from the seat of the person bringing the claim; Part 10A from the seat of the guest,
-- Part 10B from the seat of the employee who is sued.
--
-- Every judgment the Act hands to the court ("reasonable", "obvious", "satisfied", "considers just") is an input. The
-- answers say what the Act lets or stops; JUST FALSE is the Act's own no ("does not breach ... unless", "is not liable",
-- "not entitled ... unless", "There is no liability ... unless", "does not owe a duty"); NOTHING is the Act's silence or
-- a case outside it, left to the general law.
--
-- Copyright (c) State of Tasmania. Quoted from an unofficial plain-text copy under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- Based on material from the Tasmanian Legislation website at 6 November 2026.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

-- Part 4 takes no accrual date from s 4 (only s 4(1)): the date passed is a placeholder (1 January 2000) no rule reads.

§ `Part 4 -- Apologies (s 7)`

-- s 7(1): an apology "(a) does not constitute an express or implied admission of fault or liability by the person in
-- connection with that matter; and (b) is not relevant to the determination of fault or liability". (2): evidence of it
-- "is not admissible in any civil proceedings as evidence of the fault or liability". (3): "This section does not apply
-- to an apology in respect of child abuse." s 6B: "apology means an expression of sympathy or regret, or of a general
-- sense of benevolence or compassion, which does not contain an admission of fault or liability". An apology by an
-- organisation for child abuse is s 7AA (clatas-abuse.l4).
@export Can the defendant's apology be used as evidence that they were at fault (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 6B, 7)? No: an expression of sympathy or regret that does not admit fault is not an admission and is not admissible as evidence of fault or liability; but s 7 does not cover an apology for child abuse. Answers false where s 7 applies, nothing otherwise.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `it accrued before the Act commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did your cause of action accrue before the Civil Liability Act 2002 commenced (a day proclaimed under s 2)?
      `the apology admits fault or liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the apology contain an admission of fault or liability (not just sympathy or regret)?
      `the apology is for child abuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the apology in respect of child abuse (sexual or physical abuse of a child)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the apology be used as evidence of fault` k `it accrued before the Act commenced` `the apology admits fault or liability` `the apology is for child abuse` MEANS
    IF          `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 4 (apologies)` k (`the calendar date` 1 1 2000) `it accrued before the Act commenced`
            AND (NOT `the apology admits fault or liability`)
            AND (NOT `the apology is for child abuse`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Part 6 -- Breach of duty and causation (ss 11, 13)`

DECLARE `What ss 11 and 13 say` IS ONE OF
    `Part 6 does not apply to my claim`
    `no breach: the risk was not foreseeable (s 11(1)(a))`
    `no breach: the risk was insignificant (s 11(1)(b))`
    `no breach: a reasonable person would not have taken precautions (s 11(1)(c))`
    `no causation: the breach was not a necessary element of the harm (s 13(1)(a))`
    `no causation: the harm is outside the scope of liability (s 13(1)(b))`
    `ss 11 and 13 do not stand in the way of my claim`

-- s 11(1): "A person does not breach a duty to take reasonable care unless -- (a) there was a foreseeable risk of harm
-- (that is, a risk of harm of which the person knew or ought reasonably to have known); and (b) the risk was not
-- insignificant; and (c) in the circumstances, a reasonable person in the position of the person would have taken
-- precautions to avoid the risk." s 13(1): "Prerequisites for a decision that a breach of duty caused particular harm
-- are as follows: (a) the breach of duty was a necessary element of the occurrence of the harm ("factual causation");
-- (b) it is appropriate for the scope of the liability of the person in breach to extend to the harm so caused ("scope
-- of liability")." (2): in an exceptional case the court may treat a breach as satisfying (1)(a). s 14: the plaintiff
-- bears the onus of proving any fact relevant to causation.
@export Do the Act's tests for breach of duty and causation stand in the way of my negligence claim (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 11, 13)? There is no breach unless the risk was foreseeable, not insignificant, and one a reasonable person would have taken precautions against; and the breach must be a necessary element of the harm (or treated as one in an exceptional case) with the harm within the scope of liability.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued (usually the day you were harmed). ISO date.
      `the defendant knew or ought to have known of the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the defendant know, or ought they reasonably to have known, of the risk of harm?
      `the risk was not insignificant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will the court find the risk was not insignificant?
      `a reasonable person would have taken precautions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will the court find that a reasonable person in the defendant's position would have taken precautions against the risk?
      `the breach was a necessary element of my harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the breach a necessary element of your harm happening (factual causation), or will the court treat it as one in an exceptional case under s 13(2)?
      `the harm is within the scope of liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will the court find it appropriate for the defendant's liability to extend to your harm?
GIVETH A `What ss 11 and 13 say`
`Do breach and causation stand in my way` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `the defendant knew or ought to have known of the risk` `the risk was not insignificant` `a reasonable person would have taken precautions` `the breach was a necessary element of my harm` `the harm is within the scope of liability` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the provision applies to my claim` `Part 6 Divisions 1 to 4 and 7 (breach of duty, causation, obvious risks, contributory negligence)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE)
            THEN `Part 6 does not apply to my claim`
        IF (NOT `the defendant knew or ought to have known of the risk`)
            THEN `no breach: the risk was not foreseeable (s 11(1)(a))`
        IF (NOT `the risk was not insignificant`)
            THEN `no breach: the risk was insignificant (s 11(1)(b))`
        IF (NOT `a reasonable person would have taken precautions`)
            THEN `no breach: a reasonable person would not have taken precautions (s 11(1)(c))`
        IF (NOT `the breach was a necessary element of my harm`)
            THEN `no causation: the breach was not a necessary element of the harm (s 13(1)(a))`
        IF (NOT `the harm is within the scope of liability`)
            THEN `no causation: the harm is outside the scope of liability (s 13(1)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `ss 11 and 13 do not stand in the way of my claim`

§ `Part 6 -- Obvious risks (ss 15 to 17)`

-- s 15(1): "obvious risk to a person who suffers harm is a risk that, in the circumstances, would have been obvious to a
-- reasonable person in the position of that person." Asked as a judgment of the court. s 16(1): "If, in an action for
-- damages for breach of duty causing harm, a defence of voluntary assumption of risk is raised by the defendant and the
-- risk is an obvious risk, the plaintiff is taken to have been aware of the existence of the risk unless the plaintiff
-- proves, on the balance of probabilities, that he or she was not aware of the existence of the risk."
@export Am I taken to have known about the risk that harmed me (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 16)? If the defendant says you voluntarily assumed the risk and the risk was obvious, you are taken to have been aware of it unless you prove you were not aware of that kind of risk. Answers true or false, or nothing where s 16 does not arise.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued. ISO date.
      `the defendant raises voluntary assumption of risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the defendant raise the defence that you voluntarily assumed the risk?
      `the risk was obvious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the risk have been obvious to a reasonable person in your position (including a risk that is patent or a matter of common knowledge)?
      `I prove I was not aware of that kind of risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you prove, on the balance of probabilities, that you were not aware of the type or kind of risk?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I taken to have known of the risk` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `the defendant raises voluntary assumption of risk` `the risk was obvious` `I prove I was not aware of that kind of risk` MEANS
    IF          `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 6 Divisions 1 to 4 and 7 (breach of duty, causation, obvious risks, contributory negligence)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE
            AND `the defendant raises voluntary assumption of risk`
            AND `the risk was obvious`
    THEN IF `I prove I was not aware of that kind of risk`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 17(1): "A person ("the defendant") does not owe a duty to another person ("the plaintiff") to warn of an obvious
-- risk to the plaintiff." (2): "Subsection (1) does not apply if -- (a) the plaintiff has requested advice or
-- information about the risk from the defendant; or (b) the defendant is required by a written law to warn the
-- plaintiff of the risk; or (c) the defendant is a professional, other than a medical practitioner, and the risk is a
-- risk of the death of or personal injury to the plaintiff from the provision of a professional service to the plaintiff
-- by the defendant in the defendant's professional capacity." (3): "Subsection (2) does not give rise to a presumption
-- of a duty to warn".
@export Did the defendant owe me a duty to warn me of an obvious risk (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 17)? No, unless you asked them for advice or information about the risk, a written law required them to warn you, or they are a professional (not a medical practitioner) and the risk was of your death or injury from their professional service to you; even then no duty is presumed.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued. ISO date.
      `the risk was obvious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the risk have been obvious to a reasonable person in your position?
      `I asked the defendant about the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you ask the defendant for advice or information about the risk?
      `a written law required the defendant to warn me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the defendant required by a written law to warn you of the risk?
      `the defendant is a professional other than a medical practitioner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the defendant a professional who is not a medical practitioner?
      `the risk was of my death or injury from their professional service to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, was the risk one of your death or personal injury from their professional service to you?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did the defendant owe me a duty to warn of an obvious risk` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `the risk was obvious` `I asked the defendant about the risk` `a written law required the defendant to warn me` `the defendant is a professional other than a medical practitioner` `the risk was of my death or injury from their professional service to me` MEANS
    IF          `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 6 Divisions 1 to 4 and 7 (breach of duty, causation, obvious risks, contributory negligence)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE
            AND `the risk was obvious`
            AND (NOT `I asked the defendant about the risk`)
            AND (NOT `a written law required the defendant to warn me`)
            AND (NOT (`the defendant is a professional other than a medical practitioner` AND `the risk was of my death or injury from their professional service to me`))
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Part 6 -- Dangerous recreational activities (ss 18 to 20)`

-- s 19: "dangerous recreational activity means a recreational activity that involves a significant degree of risk of
-- physical harm to a person"; "recreational activity includes any pursuit or activity engaged in for enjoyment,
-- relaxation or leisure" (an open "includes": asked as a fact). s 20(1): "A person is not liable for a breach of duty for
-- harm suffered by another person ("the plaintiff") as a result of the materialisation of an obvious risk of a dangerous
-- recreational activity engaged in by the plaintiff." (2): "whether or not the plaintiff was aware of the risk".
@export Can I hold the defendant liable for harm I suffered when an obvious risk of a dangerous recreational activity I was doing materialised (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 19, 20)? No: no one is liable for such harm, whether or not you were aware of the risk. Answers false where s 20 applies, nothing otherwise.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued. ISO date.
      `I was engaged in a recreational activity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you engaged in a recreational activity (a pursuit or activity for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure)?
      `it involved a significant degree of risk of physical harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will the court find the activity involved a significant degree of risk of physical harm?
      `my harm came from an obvious risk of the activity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did your harm result from the materialisation of a risk of the activity that would have been obvious to a reasonable person in your position?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I hold the defendant liable for a dangerous recreational activity` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `I was engaged in a recreational activity` `it involved a significant degree of risk of physical harm` `my harm came from an obvious risk of the activity` MEANS
    IF          `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 6 Divisions 5 and 6 (dangerous recreational activities, professional negligence)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE
            AND `I was engaged in a recreational activity`
            AND `it involved a significant degree of risk of physical harm`
            AND `my harm came from an obvious risk of the activity`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Part 6 -- Professional negligence (ss 21, 22)`

-- s 21(1): "A medical practitioner does not breach a duty owed to a patient to warn of risk, before the patient undergoes
-- any medical treatment (or at the time of the patient being given medical advice) that will involve or give rise to a
-- risk of personal injury to the patient, unless the medical practitioner at that time fails to give or arrange to be
-- given to the patient the following information about the risk (whether or not the patient asks for the information):
-- (a) information that a reasonable person in the patient's position would, in the circumstances, require to enable the
-- person to make a reasonably informed decision ...; (b) information that the medical practitioner knows or ought
-- reasonably to know the patient wants to be given before making the decision". (2): "This section does not apply where
-- a medical practitioner has to act promptly to avoid serious risk to the life or health of the patient and -- (a) the
-- patient is not able to hear or respond to a warning about the risk to the patient; and (b) there is not sufficient
-- time for the medical practitioner to contact a person responsible for making a decision for the patient."
@export Did my doctor breach the duty to warn me of the risk of the treatment or advice (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 21)? Only if the doctor failed to give you (or arrange for you to be given) the information a reasonable person in your position would need to decide, or the information the doctor knew or ought to have known you wanted, whether or not you asked; s 21 does not apply in an emergency where you could not hear or respond and there was no time to contact the person who decides for you.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued. ISO date.
      `the doctor had to act promptly to avoid serious risk to my life or health` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the doctor have to act promptly to avoid a serious risk to your life or health?
      `I could not hear or respond to a warning` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you unable to hear or respond to a warning about the risk?
      `there was no time to contact the person who decides for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was there not enough time to contact a person responsible for making decisions for you?
      `I was not given what a reasonable person would need to decide` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the doctor fail to give you (or arrange for you to be given) the information a reasonable person in your position would need to make a reasonably informed decision?
      `I was not given what the doctor knew or ought to have known I wanted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the doctor fail to give you information the doctor knew, or ought reasonably to have known, you wanted before deciding?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did my doctor breach the duty to warn` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `the doctor had to act promptly to avoid serious risk to my life or health` `I could not hear or respond to a warning` `there was no time to contact the person who decides for me` `I was not given what a reasonable person would need to decide` `I was not given what the doctor knew or ought to have known I wanted` MEANS
    IF      (NOT (`the provision applies to my claim` `Part 6 Divisions 5 and 6 (dangerous recreational activities, professional negligence)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE))
         OR (`the doctor had to act promptly to avoid serious risk to my life or health` AND `I could not hear or respond to a warning` AND `there was no time to contact the person who decides for me`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `I was not given what a reasonable person would need to decide` OR `I was not given what the doctor knew or ought to have known I wanted`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 22(1): "A person practising a profession ("a professional") does not breach a duty arising from the provision of a
-- professional service if it is established that the professional acted in a manner that (at the time the service was
-- provided) was widely accepted in Australia by peer professional opinion as competent professional practice." (2):
-- "Peer professional opinion cannot be relied on for the purpose of this section if the court considers that the
-- opinion is irrational." (5): "This section does not apply to liability arising in connection with the giving of (or the
-- failure to give) a warning, advice or other information in relation to the risk of harm".
@export Can my professional escape a finding of breach because they acted as their peers widely accept (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 22)? Yes, if it is established that they acted in a way widely accepted in Australia by peer professional opinion as competent practice at the time, unless the court considers that opinion irrational; s 22 does not cover warnings, advice or information about risk. Answers false (no breach) where s 22 protects them, nothing otherwise.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued. ISO date.
      `my claim is about a warning, advice or information on risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your claim about the giving of, or failure to give, a warning, advice or other information about the risk of harm?
      `it is established they acted as widely accepted by peers` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it established that the professional acted in a manner widely accepted in Australia, at the time, by peer professional opinion as competent professional practice?
      `the court considers that peer opinion irrational` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the court consider that peer professional opinion irrational?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my professional be found in breach despite peer opinion` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `my claim is about a warning, advice or information on risk` `it is established they acted as widely accepted by peers` `the court considers that peer opinion irrational` MEANS
    IF          `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 6 Divisions 5 and 6 (dangerous recreational activities, professional negligence)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE
            AND (NOT `my claim is about a warning, advice or information on risk`)
            AND `it is established they acted as widely accepted by peers`
            AND (NOT `the court considers that peer opinion irrational`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Part 8 -- Mental harm (ss 29 to 35)`

-- s 32(3): "close member of the family of a victim means -- (a) a parent of the victim or other person with parental
-- responsibility for the victim; or (b) the spouse of the victim; or (c) a child or stepchild of the victim or any other
-- person for whom the victim has parental responsibility; or (d) a brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister, or
-- stepbrother or stepsister of the victim"; "spouse means -- (a) a husband or wife; or (b) a de facto spouse -- but where
-- more than one person would so qualify as a spouse, means only the last person to so qualify."
DECLARE `My relationship to the victim` IS ONE OF
    `parent, or another person with parental responsibility for the victim`
    `husband, wife or de facto spouse (the last to qualify, if more than one)`
    `an earlier husband, wife or de facto spouse, where a later one also qualifies`
    `child or stepchild, or a person for whom the victim has parental responsibility`
    `brother, sister, half-brother, half-sister, stepbrother or stepsister`
    `another relationship, or none`

@export Am I a close member of the family of the person who was killed, injured or put in peril (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 32(3))? A parent or other person with parental responsibility; the spouse (the last to qualify, if more than one); a child, stepchild or person the victim has parental responsibility for; a brother, sister, half-sibling or step-sibling.
GIVEN r IS A `My relationship to the victim` @desc Your relationship to the person who was killed, injured or put in peril.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I a close member of the victim's family` r MEANS
    CONSIDER r
    WHEN `an earlier husband, wife or de facto spouse, where a later one also qualifies` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `another relationship, or none` THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

DECLARE `What I witnessed at the scene` IS ONE OF
    `the victim being killed, injured or put in peril`
    `the immediate aftermath of the victim being killed or injured`
    `the immediate aftermath of the victim being put in peril, without being killed or injured`
    `nothing at the scene`

GIVEN w IS A `What I witnessed at the scene`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I witnessed what s 32(2)(a) needs` w IF
    CONSIDER w
    WHEN `the victim being killed, injured or put in peril` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the immediate aftermath of the victim being killed or injured` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 32(2): "The plaintiff is not entitled to recover damages for pure mental harm unless -- (a) the plaintiff witnessed,
-- at the scene, the victim being killed, injured or put in peril or the immediate aftermath of the victim being killed
-- or injured; or (b) the plaintiff is a close member of the family of the victim." s 33: "There is no liability to pay
-- damages for pure mental harm resulting from breach of duty unless the harm consists of a recognised psychiatric
-- illness." s 34(1): "A person ("the defendant") does not owe a duty to another person ("the plaintiff") to take care not
-- to cause the plaintiff mental harm unless a reasonable person in the position of the defendant ought to have foreseen
-- that a person of normal fortitude might, in the circumstances of the case, suffer a recognised psychiatric illness if
-- reasonable care were not taken." s 29: "pure mental harm means mental harm other than consequential mental harm".
@export Can I recover damages for pure mental harm from the shock of someone else being killed, injured or put in peril (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 32 to 34)? Only if your harm is a recognised psychiatric illness, the defendant ought to have foreseen that a person of normal fortitude might suffer one, and either you witnessed it at the scene (or the immediate aftermath of a death or injury) or you are a close family member. Answers false where the Act says no, nothing where it leaves the claim to the general law.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued (usually the day of the shock). ISO date.
      `my harm is a recognised psychiatric illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your mental harm a recognised psychiatric illness?
      w IS A `What I witnessed at the scene` @desc What you witnessed, at the scene.
      r IS A `My relationship to the victim` @desc Your relationship to the person who was killed, injured or put in peril.
      `a person of normal fortitude might foreseeably have suffered a psychiatric illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will the court find a reasonable person in the defendant's position ought to have foreseen that a person of normal fortitude might, in the circumstances, suffer a recognised psychiatric illness if reasonable care were not taken?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I recover for pure mental harm from shock` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `my harm is a recognised psychiatric illness` w r `a person of normal fortitude might foreseeably have suffered a psychiatric illness` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the provision applies to my claim` `Part 8 (mental harm)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF     `my harm is a recognised psychiatric illness`
            AND (`I witnessed what s 32(2)(a) needs` w OR `Am I a close member of the victim's family` r EQUALS JUST TRUE)
            AND `a person of normal fortitude might foreseeably have suffered a psychiatric illness`
         THEN NOTHING
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 35: "A court cannot make an award of damages for economic loss for consequential mental harm resulting from breach of
-- duty unless the harm consists of a recognised psychiatric illness." s 29: "consequential mental harm means mental harm
-- that is a consequence of a personal injury of any other kind". s 34 applies to it too, with "the nature and extent of
-- personal injury suffered" among the circumstances (s 34(3)).
@export Can I be awarded damages for economic loss caused by mental harm that followed my physical injury (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 34, 35)? Only if the mental harm is a recognised psychiatric illness and the defendant ought to have foreseen that a person of normal fortitude might suffer one. Answers false where the Act says no, nothing where it leaves the claim to the general law.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued (usually the day you were injured). ISO date.
      `my harm is a recognised psychiatric illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your mental harm a recognised psychiatric illness?
      `a person of normal fortitude might foreseeably have suffered a psychiatric illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will the court find a reasonable person in the defendant's position ought to have foreseen that a person of normal fortitude might, given your injury, suffer a recognised psychiatric illness if reasonable care were not taken?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I recover economic loss for consequential mental harm` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `my harm is a recognised psychiatric illness` `a person of normal fortitude might foreseeably have suffered a psychiatric illness` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the provision applies to my claim` `Part 8 (mental harm)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `my harm is a recognised psychiatric illness` AND `a person of normal fortitude might foreseeably have suffered a psychiatric illness`
         THEN NOTHING
         ELSE JUST FALSE

§ `Part 8A -- Good samaritans (ss 35B, 35C)`

-- s 35B(1): "A good samaritan is an individual who provides assistance, advice or care to another person in relation to
-- an emergency or accident in circumstances in which -- (a) he or she expects no money or other financial reward for
-- providing the assistance, advice or care; and (b) as a result of the emergency or accident the person ... is ill, is at
-- risk of death or injury, is injured, is apparently ill, is apparently at risk of death or injury or is apparently
-- injured." (2): "A good samaritan is not liable in any civil proceeding for anything done, or not done, by him or her in
-- good faith and without recklessness -- (a) in providing assistance, advice or care at the scene of the emergency or
-- accident; or (b) in providing advice by telephone or by another means of communication to a person at the scene". (3):
-- even if the good samaritan caused the emergency. (4): not "to any act or omission ... that occurs before the
-- assistance, advice or care is provided". s 35C(1): no protection if "(a) the ability of the good samaritan to exercise
-- reasonable care and skill was significantly impaired by reason of the good samaritan being under the influence of
-- alcohol or a drug voluntarily consumed ...; and (b) the good samaritan failed to exercise reasonable care and skill".
-- (2): none while "impersonating a health care or emergency services worker or a police officer or ... falsely
-- representing that the person has skills or expertise".
@export Can I hold liable the person who helped me in an emergency or accident (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 35B, 35C)? Not if they were an individual expecting no reward, you were (or seemed) ill, injured or at risk, and they acted in good faith and without recklessness at the scene or by phone to the scene; unless they were significantly impaired by alcohol or drugs they chose to take and failed to take reasonable care, or were posing as a health, emergency or police worker or falsely claiming expertise. Answers false where they are protected, nothing otherwise.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued. ISO date.
      `the helper is an individual expecting no reward` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the helper an individual who expected no money or other financial reward for helping?
      `I was or seemed ill, injured or at risk from the emergency` IS A BOOLEAN @desc As a result of the emergency or accident, were you (or did you appear to be) ill, injured, or at risk of death or injury?
      `they acted in good faith and without recklessness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the helper act in good faith and without recklessness?
      `they helped at the scene or advised someone at the scene from afar` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what they did done in helping at the scene, or in giving advice by phone or other means to a person at the scene?
      `the act complained of came before they began to help` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the act or omission you complain of happen before they began to help (for example, causing the accident)?
      `they were significantly impaired by alcohol or drugs they chose to take and failed to take reasonable care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the helper's ability significantly impaired by alcohol or a drug they voluntarily consumed, and did they fail to exercise reasonable care and skill?
      `they were impersonating a health, emergency or police worker or falsely claiming expertise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the helper impersonating a health care or emergency services worker or a police officer, or falsely representing skills or expertise in emergency help?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I hold the good samaritan liable` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `the helper is an individual expecting no reward` `I was or seemed ill, injured or at risk from the emergency` `they acted in good faith and without recklessness` `they helped at the scene or advised someone at the scene from afar` `the act complained of came before they began to help` `they were significantly impaired by alcohol or drugs they chose to take and failed to take reasonable care` `they were impersonating a health, emergency or police worker or falsely claiming expertise` MEANS
    IF          `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 8A (good samaritans)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE
            AND `the helper is an individual expecting no reward`
            AND `I was or seemed ill, injured or at risk from the emergency`
            AND `they acted in good faith and without recklessness`
            AND `they helped at the scene or advised someone at the scene from afar`
            AND (NOT `the act complained of came before they began to help`)
            AND (NOT `they were significantly impaired by alcohol or drugs they chose to take and failed to take reasonable care`)
            AND (NOT `they were impersonating a health, emergency or police worker or falsely claiming expertise`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Part 8B -- Food donors (s 35F)`

-- s 35F(1): "A person who donates food (the "food donor") does not incur any civil liability in respect of any death or
-- personal injury that results from the consumption of the food if -- (a) the food donor donated the food -- (i) in good
-- faith for a charitable or benevolent purpose; and (ii) with the intention that the consumer of the food would not have
-- to pay for the food; and (b) the food was safe to consume at the time it left the possession or control of the food
-- donor; and (c) where the food was of a nature that required it to be handled in a particular way to ensure that it
-- remained safe to consume ..., the food donor informed the person to whom the food donor gave the food of those
-- handling requirements; and (d) where the food would only have remained safe to consume for a particular period of
-- time ..., the food donor informed the person to whom the food donor gave the food of that time limit."
@export Can I hold a food donor liable for my illness or injury from eating donated food (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 35F)? Not if they donated it in good faith for a charitable or benevolent purpose, meaning it to be free, it was safe when it left them, and they passed on any handling requirements and any time limit for safe eating. Answers false where the donor is protected, nothing otherwise.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued. ISO date.
      `donated in good faith for a charitable or benevolent purpose, to be free` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the donor give the food in good faith for a charitable or benevolent purpose, meaning that the consumer would not have to pay?
      `the food was safe when it left the donor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the food safe to consume (not unsafe under the Food Act 2003) when it left the donor's possession or control?
      `the food needed particular handling to stay safe` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the food need to be handled in a particular way to stay safe to consume?
      `the donor passed on the handling requirements` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, did the donor tell the person they gave it to about those handling requirements?
      `the food was safe only for a limited time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the food only have stayed safe to consume for a particular period?
      `the donor passed on the time limit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, did the donor tell the person they gave it to about that time limit?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I hold the food donor liable` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `donated in good faith for a charitable or benevolent purpose, to be free` `the food was safe when it left the donor` `the food needed particular handling to stay safe` `the donor passed on the handling requirements` `the food was safe only for a limited time` `the donor passed on the time limit` MEANS
    IF          `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 8B (food donors)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE
            AND `donated in good faith for a charitable or benevolent purpose, to be free`
            AND `the food was safe when it left the donor`
            AND ((NOT `the food needed particular handling to stay safe`) OR `the donor passed on the handling requirements`)
            AND ((NOT `the food was safe only for a limited time`) OR `the donor passed on the time limit`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Part 9 -- Public and other authorities (ss 39 to 42)`

-- s 39(1): "A public or other authority ("the authority") does not owe a duty to a person who engages in a recreational
-- activity to take care in respect of a risk of the activity if the risk was the subject of a risk warning to that
-- person." (2): for "an incapable person" the authority may rely on a warning only to another capable person caring for
-- or accompanying them, or to a parent. (3): a risk warning "is a warning that is given in a manner that is reasonably
-- likely to result in people being warned of the risk before engaging in the recreational activity". (7): "not entitled
-- to rely on a risk warning unless it is given by or on behalf of the authority or has been adopted by the authority".
-- (8): not if the harm "resulted from a contravention of a provision of a written law ... that establishes specific
-- practices or procedures for the protection of personal safety". (9): not "to the extent that the warning was
-- contradicted by any representation as to risk made by or on behalf of the authority". (10): not "if the person was
-- required to engage in the recreational activity by the authority". (11): not if the harm "resulted from an act done or
-- omission made with reckless disregard for the consequences".
@export Did the public authority owe me a duty of care for a risk of the recreational activity I was doing, given it warned of the risk (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 39)? No, if the risk was the subject of a risk warning given by or for the authority in a way reasonably likely to warn people beforehand (for a child or a person without capacity, to the person with them or a parent), unless the harm came from breach of a safety law, the authority contradicted the warning, required you to take part, or acted with reckless disregard. Answers false where there is no duty, nothing otherwise.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued. ISO date.
      `the defendant is a public or other authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the defendant a public or other authority (the Crown, a council, a statutory authority, a State-owned company, a water corporation or regulated entity, or a prescribed body)?
      `I was engaged in a recreational activity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you engaged in a recreational activity (a pursuit or activity for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure)?
      `I lacked capacity to understand the warning` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Because of your young age or a disability, did you lack the capacity to understand the risk warning?
      `the risk was warned of to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the risk the subject of a warning to you, given orally or in writing (including a sign) in a way reasonably likely to warn people before they took part?
      `the risk was warned of to the capable person with me or to my parent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you lacked capacity: was the risk the subject of such a warning to a capable person caring for or accompanying you (not the authority), or to your parent?
      `the warning was given or adopted by the authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the warning given by or on behalf of the authority, or adopted by it?
      `the harm came from breach of a personal safety law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it established that your harm resulted from a breach of a written law setting specific practices or procedures for personal safety?
      `the authority contradicted the warning` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the authority (or someone for it) make a representation to you about the risk that contradicted the warning?
      `the authority required me to take part` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the authority require you to engage in the activity?
      `the harm came from reckless disregard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it established that your harm resulted from an act or omission done with reckless disregard for the consequences?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did the authority owe me a duty despite its risk warning` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `the defendant is a public or other authority` `I was engaged in a recreational activity` `I lacked capacity to understand the warning` `the risk was warned of to me` `the risk was warned of to the capable person with me or to my parent` `the warning was given or adopted by the authority` `the harm came from breach of a personal safety law` `the authority contradicted the warning` `the authority required me to take part` `the harm came from reckless disregard` MEANS
    IF          `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 9 (public and other authorities)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE
            AND `the defendant is a public or other authority`
            AND `I was engaged in a recreational activity`
            AND `the warning counts`
            AND `the warning was given or adopted by the authority`
            AND (NOT `the harm came from breach of a personal safety law`)
            AND (NOT `the authority contradicted the warning`)
            AND (NOT `the authority required me to take part`)
            AND (NOT `the harm came from reckless disregard`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING
    WHERE
        `the warning counts` MEANS
            IF `I lacked capacity to understand the warning`
            THEN `the risk was warned of to the capable person with me or to my parent`
            ELSE `the risk was warned of to me`

-- s 40(2): "an act or omission of the authority does not constitute a breach of statutory duty unless the act or
-- omission was in the circumstances so unreasonable that no authority having the functions of the authority in question
-- could properly consider the act or omission to be a reasonable exercise of its functions." s 41(1): "A public or other
-- authority is not liable ... to the extent that the claim is based on the failure of the authority to exercise or to
-- consider exercising any function of the authority to prohibit or regulate an activity if the authority could not have
-- been required to exercise the function in proceedings instituted by the claimant." (2): issuing a licence, permit or
-- registration is a function to regulate.
@export Can I hold a public authority liable for breach of a statutory duty, or for failing to prohibit or regulate an activity (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 40, 41)? Its act or omission is not a breach of statutory duty unless it was so unreasonable that no authority with its functions could properly consider it reasonable; and it is not liable for failing to use a power to prohibit or regulate (including licensing) unless you could have made it use that power in proceedings. Answers false where the Act says no, nothing otherwise.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued. ISO date.
      `my claim is for breach of a statutory duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your claim based on the authority breaching a statutory duty in exercising, or failing to exercise, its functions?
      `it was so unreasonable no authority could properly think it reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will the court find the act or omission was so unreasonable that no authority with those functions could properly consider it a reasonable exercise of them?
      `my claim is for failing to prohibit or regulate an activity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your claim based on the authority failing to exercise (or consider exercising) a power to prohibit or regulate an activity (including issuing a licence, permit or registration)?
      `I could have made it exercise that power in proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Could the authority have been required to exercise that power in proceedings brought by you?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I hold the authority liable for breach of statutory duty or failure to regulate` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `my claim is for breach of a statutory duty` `it was so unreasonable no authority could properly think it reasonable` `my claim is for failing to prohibit or regulate an activity` `I could have made it exercise that power in proceedings` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the provision applies to my claim` `Part 9 (public and other authorities)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF    (`my claim is for breach of a statutory duty` AND (NOT `it was so unreasonable no authority could properly think it reasonable`))
            OR (`my claim is for failing to prohibit or regulate an activity` AND (NOT `I could have made it exercise that power in proceedings`))
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE NOTHING

-- s 42(1): "A public or other authority responsible for carrying out road work is not liable in proceedings in respect of
-- a claim to which this Part applies for harm arising from a failure of the authority to carry out road work, or to
-- consider carrying out road work, unless at the time of the alleged failure the authority had actual knowledge of the
-- facts creating the particular risk the materialisation of which resulted in the harm." (2): actual knowledge does not
-- itself create a duty. (3): "road" includes footpaths, bridges and any place open to the public.
@export Can I hold the road authority liable for harm from its failure to carry out road work (repair, maintenance, inspection) (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 42)? Not unless, at the time of the failure, it actually knew of the facts creating the particular risk that caused your harm; knowledge alone does not create a duty. Answers false where s 42 says no, nothing otherwise.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued. ISO date.
      `my claim is for a failure to carry out road work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your claim for harm from the authority failing to carry out (or consider carrying out) road work on a road, footpath, bridge or other public place?
      `the authority actually knew of the facts creating the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc At the time of the failure, did the authority have actual knowledge of the facts creating the particular risk that caused your harm?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I hold the road authority liable` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `my claim is for a failure to carry out road work` `the authority actually knew of the facts creating the risk` MEANS
    IF          `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 9 (public and other authorities)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE
            AND `my claim is for a failure to carry out road work`
            AND (NOT `the authority actually knew of the facts creating the risk`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Part 9A -- Proportionate liability (ss 43A, 43B, 43E)`

-- s 43A(1): apportionable claims are "(a) a claim for economic loss or damage to property in an action for damages
-- (whether in contract, tort or otherwise) arising from a failure to take reasonable care (but not including any claim
-- arising out of personal injury); (b) a claim for economic loss or damage to property in an action for damages under
-- the Australian Consumer Law (Tasmania) for a contravention of section 236 of that Act." (3): limited to those. (5):
-- nothing limits a concurrent wrongdoer who "(a) ... intended to cause" or "(b) ... fraudulently caused" the loss. s
-- 43B(1): the liability of a concurrent wrongdoer "is limited to an amount reflecting that proportion of the damage or
-- loss claimed that the court considers just"; (3)(a): the court excludes the plaintiff's own contributory share.
DECLARE `My claim for Part 9A` IS ONE OF
    `economic loss or property damage from a failure to take reasonable care, not arising from personal injury`
    `economic loss or property damage under the Australian Consumer Law (Tasmania) for a contravention of s 236`
    `a claim arising out of personal injury, or another claim`

@export How much can I recover from one of several wrongdoers for my economic loss or property damage (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 43A, 43B)? For an apportionable claim (failure to take reasonable care, not personal injury, or Australian Consumer Law s 236), each concurrent wrongdoer is liable only for the share of the loss the court considers just, unless that wrongdoer intended to cause the loss or caused it fraudulently. Answers the dollar amount, or nothing where Part 9A does not limit it.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued. ISO date.
      c IS A `My claim for Part 9A` @desc What your claim is for.
      `this wrongdoer intended the loss or caused it fraudulently` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did this wrongdoer intend to cause the loss or damage, or cause it fraudulently?
      `the loss claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The loss or damage you claim, in dollars, after excluding any share for your own contributory negligence.
      `the share the court considers just` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage of the loss the court considers just for this wrongdoer's responsibility (for example 40 for 40%).
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much can I recover from this concurrent wrongdoer` k `the day my cause of action accrued` c `this wrongdoer intended the loss or caused it fraudulently` `the loss claimed` `the share the court considers just` MEANS
    IF          `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 9A (proportionate liability)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE
            AND `it is an apportionable claim`
            AND (NOT `this wrongdoer intended the loss or caused it fraudulently`)
    THEN JUST (`the loss claimed` TIMES `the share the court considers just` DIVIDED BY 100)
    ELSE NOTHING
    WHERE
        `it is an apportionable claim` MEANS
            CONSIDER c
            WHEN `a claim arising out of personal injury, or another claim` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 43E(1): a plaintiff who recovered judgment against one concurrent wrongdoer may sue another for the same loss. (2):
-- "the plaintiff cannot recover an amount of damages that, having regard to any damages previously recovered by the
-- plaintiff in respect of the damage or loss, would result in the plaintiff receiving compensation for damage or loss
-- that is greater than the damage or loss actually sustained by the plaintiff."
@export After recovering part of my loss from one wrongdoer, how much more can I recover from another (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 43E)? You may sue another concurrent wrongdoer, but not so as to be compensated for more than the loss you actually sustained. Answers the most you can still recover.
GIVEN `the loss I actually sustained` IS A NUMBER @desc The loss or damage you actually sustained, in dollars.
      `what I have already recovered` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages you have already recovered for that loss, in dollars.
      `what this action would award` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages the court would otherwise award against this wrongdoer, in dollars.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much more can I recover from another wrongdoer` `the loss I actually sustained` `what I have already recovered` `what this action would award` MEANS
    IF `what I have already recovered` AT LEAST `the loss I actually sustained`
    THEN 0
    ELSE IF `what this action would award` AT MOST (`the loss I actually sustained` MINUS `what I have already recovered`)
         THEN `what this action would award`
         ELSE `the loss I actually sustained` MINUS `what I have already recovered`

§ `Part 10 -- Volunteers (ss 44 to 49)`

-- s 45(1): "volunteer means a person who does community work on a voluntary basis but does not include a person who is
-- -- (a) taken to be a worker for the purposes of the Workers Rehabilitation and Compensation Act 1988 or the
-- Asbestos-Related Diseases (Occupational Exposure) Compensation Act 2011; or (b) performing a function prescribed by the
-- regulations." (2): on a voluntary basis if the person receives no remuneration other than what they would get anyway
-- or reimbursement of reasonable expenses, or no more than any prescribed amount (none is deposited). (3): not under a
-- court order. s 44: "community work" is work organised by a community organisation for the listed purposes.
GIVEN `they did community work organised by a community organisation` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `they were unpaid beyond expenses, pay they would get anyway or a prescribed amount` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `they were working under a court order` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `they are a worker under the workers compensation or asbestos diseases Acts, or in a prescribed function` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `they are a volunteer (s 45)` `they did community work organised by a community organisation` `they were unpaid beyond expenses, pay they would get anyway or a prescribed amount` `they were working under a court order` `they are a worker under the workers compensation or asbestos diseases Acts, or in a prescribed function` IF
        `they did community work organised by a community organisation`
    AND `they were unpaid beyond expenses, pay they would get anyway or a prescribed amount`
    AND (NOT `they were working under a court order`)
    AND (NOT `they are a worker under the workers compensation or asbestos diseases Acts, or in a prescribed function`)

-- s 47(1): "a volunteer does not incur civil liability for anything that the volunteer has done in good faith when doing
-- community work." (2): it "does not affect any right to recover damages in respect of defamation or in respect of the
-- death of, or personal injury to, any person directly caused by, or by the driving of, a motor vehicle if ... (a) the
-- vehicle was owned or being driven by a person who, but for ... subsection (1), would incur liability ...; and (b) the
-- vehicle -- (i) was a vehicle in respect of which a premium had been paid, or had been required to be paid, in
-- accordance with Part V of the Motor Accidents (Liabilities and Compensation) Act 1973; or (ii) was a "permitted
-- out-of-State vehicle" ... in respect of which a third party insurance policy was in force". (3): no protection for a
-- volunteer "(a) who knew or ought reasonably to have known that at the relevant time he or she was acting -- (i) outside
-- the scope of the community work ...; or (ii) contrary to instructions ...; or (b) whose ability to do the community
-- work in a proper manner was, at the relevant time, significantly impaired by alcohol or drugs". s 48(1): "A community
-- organisation incurs the civil liability that, but for the operation of section 47(1), a volunteer would incur".
DECLARE `The volunteer's answer` IS ONE OF
    `the volunteer is not liable; the community organisation that principally organised the work takes on the liability (ss 47(1), 48(1))`
    `Part 10 does not protect the volunteer`

@export Can I hold a volunteer personally liable for what they did while doing community work, or must I sue the community organisation (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 45, 47, 48)? A volunteer acting in good faith in community work is not liable, and the organisation that principally organised the work takes on the liability; but not for defamation, for injury caused by an insured motor vehicle they owned or drove, if they knew or ought to have known they were outside the work or against instructions, or if alcohol or drugs significantly impaired them.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued (the day of the volunteer's act). ISO date.
      `they did community work organised by a community organisation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person doing community work organised (directed or supervised) by a community organisation (a State Service Agency, statutory authority, incorporated association, council or body corporate)?
      `they were unpaid beyond expenses, pay they would get anyway or a prescribed amount` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did they receive no pay for it other than pay they would get anyway, reimbursement of reasonable expenses, or no more than any prescribed amount?
      `they were working under a court order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were they doing the work under a court order?
      `they are a worker under the workers compensation or asbestos diseases Acts, or in a prescribed function` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are they taken to be a worker under the Workers Rehabilitation and Compensation Act 1988 or the Asbestos-Related Diseases (Occupational Exposure) Compensation Act 2011, or performing a prescribed function?
      `they acted in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did they act in good faith?
      `my claim is for defamation or injury from an insured motor vehicle they owned or drove` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your claim for defamation, or for death or injury caused by (or by the driving of) a motor vehicle they owned or drove on which the compulsory third party premium was paid or payable (or an insured permitted out-of-State vehicle)?
      `they knew or ought to have known they were outside the work or against instructions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did they know, or ought they reasonably to have known, that they were acting outside the community work or contrary to the organisation's instructions?
      `alcohol or drugs significantly impaired them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was their ability to do the work properly significantly impaired by alcohol or by drugs taken voluntarily and not for therapeutic purposes?
GIVETH A `The volunteer's answer`
`Can I hold the volunteer liable` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `they did community work organised by a community organisation` `they were unpaid beyond expenses, pay they would get anyway or a prescribed amount` `they were working under a court order` `they are a worker under the workers compensation or asbestos diseases Acts, or in a prescribed function` `they acted in good faith` `my claim is for defamation or injury from an insured motor vehicle they owned or drove` `they knew or ought to have known they were outside the work or against instructions` `alcohol or drugs significantly impaired them` MEANS
    IF          `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 10 (volunteers)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE
            AND `they are a volunteer (s 45)` `they did community work organised by a community organisation` `they were unpaid beyond expenses, pay they would get anyway or a prescribed amount` `they were working under a court order` `they are a worker under the workers compensation or asbestos diseases Acts, or in a prescribed function`
            AND `they acted in good faith`
            AND (NOT `my claim is for defamation or injury from an insured motor vehicle they owned or drove`)
            AND (NOT `they knew or ought to have known they were outside the work or against instructions`)
            AND (NOT `alcohol or drugs significantly impaired them`)
    THEN `the volunteer is not liable; the community organisation that principally organised the work takes on the liability (ss 47(1), 48(1))`
    ELSE `Part 10 does not protect the volunteer`

§ `Part 10A -- Accommodation providers (s 49A)`

-- s 49A(2): "An accommodation provider is not liable to make compensation in relation to the loss, destruction or damage
-- of or to property brought to tourist accommodation premises by or on behalf of a guest except as provided in
-- subsection (3)." (3): liable if "(a) the loss, destruction or damage is attributable to some negligence or deliberate
-- or reckless act or default of the accommodation provider or an employee ...; or (b) the property -- (i) has been
-- deposited expressly for safe custody with the accommodation provider or an employee ... authorised to accept guest's
-- property for safe custody; and (ii) if the accommodation provider or that employee requires it, has been placed in a
-- container and fastened or sealed by the person depositing the property for safe custody; and (iii) has been lost,
-- destroyed or damaged while so deposited; or (c) a notice that relates to the liability of an accommodation provider
-- to pay compensation under this section is not conspicuously displayed in, at or near the reception office, reception
-- desk or principal entrance". (1): "guest means a person -- (a) who is temporarily absent from his or her usual
-- residence; and (b) who has engaged sleeping accommodation ... at tourist accommodation premises".
@export Must the hotel or other tourist accommodation compensate me for my property that was lost, destroyed or damaged there (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 49A)? Only if the loss was due to negligence or a deliberate or reckless act of the provider or its staff; or you deposited it expressly for safe custody (sealed in a container if asked) and it was lost while deposited; or the provider did not conspicuously display the notice about its liability at reception or the main entrance.
GIVEN `I was a guest away from home with sleeping accommodation booked there` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you temporarily away from your usual residence, with sleeping accommodation engaged (by you or for you) at premises set up to give overnight lodging to tourists or travellers for payment?
      `the loss was due to negligence or a deliberate or reckless act of the provider or staff` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the loss, destruction or damage attributable to negligence, or a deliberate or reckless act or default, of the provider or an employee?
      `I deposited it expressly for safe custody with the provider or authorised staff` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you deposit the property expressly for safe custody with the provider or an employee authorised to accept it?
      `I sealed it in a container if asked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the provider or employee required it, did you place it in a container fastened or sealed by you? (Answer yes if it was not required.)
      `it was lost, destroyed or damaged while deposited` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it lost, destroyed or damaged while so deposited?
      `the liability notice was conspicuously displayed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was a notice about the provider's liability to pay compensation conspicuously displayed in, at or near the reception office, reception desk or principal entrance?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the accommodation provider compensate me` `I was a guest away from home with sleeping accommodation booked there` `the loss was due to negligence or a deliberate or reckless act of the provider or staff` `I deposited it expressly for safe custody with the provider or authorised staff` `I sealed it in a container if asked` `it was lost, destroyed or damaged while deposited` `the liability notice was conspicuously displayed` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I was a guest away from home with sleeping accommodation booked there`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF    `the loss was due to negligence or a deliberate or reckless act of the provider or staff`
            OR (`I deposited it expressly for safe custody with the provider or authorised staff` AND `I sealed it in a container if asked` AND `it was lost, destroyed or damaged while deposited`)
            OR (NOT `the liability notice was conspicuously displayed`)
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

§ `Part 10B -- Employer and employee (s 49B)`

-- s 49B(1): "If an employee commits a tort for which his or her employer is also liable -- (a) the employee is not liable
-- to indemnify, or to pay any contribution to, the employer in respect of the liability incurred by the employer; and
-- (b) the employer is liable to indemnify the employee in respect of liability incurred by the employee for the tort
-- (unless the employee is otherwise entitled to an indemnity in respect of that liability)." (3): "This section does not
-- apply to a tort committed by an employee if the conduct constituting the tort -- (a) was serious and wilful misconduct;
-- or (b) did not occur in the course of, and did not arise out of, the employment of the employee." s 4(6): only a cause
-- of action accrued after Part 10B commenced.
DECLARE `My employer and I` IS ONE OF
    `my employer must indemnify me, and I need not indemnify or contribute to my employer (s 49B(1))`
    `I need not indemnify or contribute to my employer; I am otherwise entitled to an indemnity (s 49B(1))`
    `s 49B does not apply`

@export If I am sued for a tort I committed at work and my employer is also liable, must my employer indemnify me, and can my employer make me contribute (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 49B)? Your employer must indemnify you (unless you are otherwise entitled to an indemnity) and cannot make you indemnify or contribute, unless the conduct was serious and wilful misconduct or was neither in the course of nor arising out of your employment.
GIVEN `the day the cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day the cause of action against you accrued. ISO date.
      `my employer is also liable for the tort` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your employer also liable for the tort (for example, vicariously)?
      `the conduct was serious and wilful misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your conduct serious and wilful misconduct?
      `the conduct was in the course of or arose out of my employment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the conduct occur in the course of, or arise out of, your employment?
      `I am otherwise entitled to an indemnity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you otherwise entitled to an indemnity for that liability (for example, under an insurance policy)?
GIVETH A `My employer and I`
`Must my employer indemnify me` `the day the cause of action accrued` `my employer is also liable for the tort` `the conduct was serious and wilful misconduct` `the conduct was in the course of or arose out of my employment` `I am otherwise entitled to an indemnity` MEANS
    IF          `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 10B (employer and employee)` `an ordinary claim: none of the s 3B exclusions` `the day the cause of action accrued` FALSE
            AND `my employer is also liable for the tort`
            AND (NOT `the conduct was serious and wilful misconduct`)
            AND `the conduct was in the course of or arose out of my employment`
    THEN IF `I am otherwise entitled to an indemnity`
         THEN `I need not indemnify or contribute to my employer; I am otherwise entitled to an indemnity (s 49B(1))`
         ELSE `my employer must indemnify me, and I need not indemnify or contribute to my employer (s 49B(1))`
    ELSE `s 49B does not apply`
